Docker Compose is a tool that lets you define and run a multi-container application using a single YAML file, instead of typing a long `docker run` command for every 
container. You describe all your services (frontend, backend, database, etc.) in one `compose.yaml` file, and then a single command starts everything together .
for documentation: https://docs.docker.com/reference/compose-file/services/#where-attributes-are-defined

A Compose file is organized around three main top-level sections :
Section	                              What it defines                                                                                                 	Example
services   	The containers that make up your app. Each service has a name, an image (or a build path), ports, environment variables, and volumes.   	web, db, api
networks	  Virtual networks that connect services. Services on the same network can reach each other by service name (Docker's built-in DNS).	    front-tier, back-tier
volumes	    Persistent storage that survives container restarts. Defined at the top level, then attached to a service.                                  	db-data

 Essential Commands
Command	                                              What it does
docker compose up                             	Creates and starts all services (attached). Add -d for background .
docker compose up --build	                      Rebuilds images before starting (use after changing a Dockerfile or code) .
docker compose down	                            Stops and removes containers and networks. Volumes survive .
docker compose down -v	                        Also deletes named volumes — data loss .
docker compose ps	                              Shows the status of running services .
docker compose logs -f      	                  Streams logs from all services (or name one: logs -f backend) .
docker compose exec <service> <cmd>	            Runs a command inside a running container .
docker compose config                           used to test the yaml file before running for errors

Here's a minimal example with a frontend and a backend:

``` a simple yaml file
services:
  yt-dlp-ubuntu:
    image: <username>/<repositoryname>  # 
    build: .   # . for current directory 
```

................................................................................
The Uni Helsinki project where frontend container stored in 1_12/ is used to connect with backend in 1_13/. in 1_14/, it copies directly from 1_12 and 1_13 to create a docker image 
with * docker build -t frontend -f Dockerfile.frontend .. # 2 build commands are run for Dockf.frontend and Dockf.backend # .. means to the image to the parent directory.
then two docker run commands is used for each frontend and backend * docker run -p 5001:5001 frontend *docker run -p 8080:8080 backend

In this exercises you had to:
1. Build each image separately with `docker build`.
2. Run each container separately with `docker run`, remembering the right `-p` flags and `ENV` values each time.
3. Manually create a network if you wanted the containers to talk to each other by name.

With Compose, all of that is declared once in the YAML:
- `build:` tells Compose to build the image from your Dockerfile (no separate `docker build` step).
- `ports:` replaces the `-p` flag.
- `environment:` replaces the `-e` flag (and is applied at container runtime).
- Compose automatically creates a network, so `backend` resolves to the backend container's IP.

Two Things to Watch
'*depends_on' controls start order, not readiness. If the backend takes 10 seconds to boot, '*depends_on' only makes sure the container has started, not that the app inside is ready.
For that you need a '*healthcheck' with '*condition: service_healthy'.

The ENV in your Dockerfile vs. environment: in Compose. The Dockerfile ENV is a build-time default; Compose's environment: overrides it at runtime. 
For React's REACT_APP_* variables, the value must be set at build time (in the Dockerfile, before npm run build), because CRA bakes it into the JS bundle.
Setting it in Compose's environment: would be too late — the bundle is already compiled.

--the yaml file will be
services:
  frontend:
    build:
      context: ../example-app #context points to the directory with the dependencies, this one is a directory under the parent directory hence the ..
      dockerfile: frontend/Dockerfile.frontend #the Dockerfile is in a directory called frontend, same logic for backend.
    ports:
      - "5001:5001"

  backend:
    build:
      context: ../example-app
      dockerfile: backend/Dockerfile.backend #same logic for frontend
    ports:
      - "8080:8080"

NOTE: Then *docker compose up --build from 1_14/ would replace both docker build commands and both docker run commands. 
The context: .. mirrors the .. in your docker build — it's needed so the Dockerfiles can COPY 1_12 and COPY 1_13 from the parent directory.

 How Services Talk to Each Other
Inside a Compose network, **you don't use `localhost` or IP addresses** to reach another service. You use the **service name** as the hostname .
- If the frontend container needs to call the backend, it uses `http://backend:8080` (where `backend` is the service name).
- Compose creates a DNS entry for each service, so the name resolves to the right container IP automatically.
- This works only for **container-to-container** traffic. The **browser** (on your host machine) still uses `localhost:<published_port>` to reach the services.

`REACT_APP_BACKEND_URL` is baked into the frontend and used by the **browser**, so it must point to a host-published port. 
But if the frontend container itself were making a server-side request to the backend, it would use the **service name**.


VOLUMES
In Docker Compose, a volume is how you handle data that needs to outlive a container. When a container is deleted and recreated, any data written to its internal filesystem vanishes with it. Volumes give that data a home outside the container's lifecycle, so databases don't lose their tables, and configuration files survive a restart.
There are two main types you'll encounter, and they behave very differently.
Type           	Syntax in compose.yaml      	Who Manages It    	  Where Data Lives	                            Best For
Named Volume	     my-data:/var/lib/data	        Docker	        Docker's storage area (hidden)	          Databases, persistent app state 
Bind Mount      	./local-dir:/app/dir	           You       	   A folder you choose on your host	         Development (live code reload), config files   

Named volumes are declared in the top-level volumes: section of your Compose file. Docker creates and manages the actual storage location, so you don't need to worry about host path conflicts or permissions. They are the recommended choice for production data because they survive docker compose down (unless you add the -v flag) .
Remember this distinction, because it's the most common way people accidentally delete data:
docker compose down → Stops and removes containers, but keeps named volumes .
docker compose down -v → Stops and removes containers and deletes named volumes (data loss) .
Bind mounts are never deleted by Docker commands—they are just folders on your host, so they're only removed if you delete them yourself.

Bind mounts create a direct link between a folder on your host machine and a path inside the container. Changes on your host appear instantly in the container, which is why they're perfect for editing code during development. However, they depend on the host's directory structure and can run into permission issues if the container's user ID doesn't match your host user's ID .


Use a volume with Docker Compose
The following example shows a single Docker Compose service with a volume: yaml file
services:
  webster:
    image: devopsdockeruh/simple-web-service
    build: .
    volumes:
    - ./log.txt:/usr/src/app/text.log # ./log.txt is the destination, /usr/src/app/text.log is the source #this one takes logs from inside the container to give outside
    container_name: logger
    
Running '*docker compose up for the first time creates a volume. Docker reuses the same volume when you run the command subsequently.


